Given circuit consists of constant resistance and ideal independent DC voltage sources. <img src="img/80b952253ef9ed5e.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> \[P_{\text{abs},1}=\frac{V^2}{R}\] If all resistances are scaled down by a factor \(10\) and all source voltages are scaled up by a factor \(10\), <img src="img/df2176274b7e62df.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> \[P_{\text{abs},2}
=\frac{(10V)^2}{R/10}
=1000\frac{V^2}{R}
=1000\,P_{\text{abs},1}\] Hence, the power dissipated in the circuit scales up by a factor of \[\boxed{1000}\]

Given, \[Q_1=6~\text{C},\qquad
Q_2=0~\text{C},\qquad
C_1=1~\text{F},\qquad
C_2=2~\text{F}\] In steady state, the voltage across both capacitors is the same. \[V_{C_1}=V_{C_2}
=
\frac{V_1C_1+V_2C_2}{C_1+C_2}
\tag{i}\] From the given data, \[Q_1=C_1V_1\] \[6=1\times V_1\] \[V_1=6~\text{V}
\tag{ii}\] Also, \[Q_2=C_2V_2\] \[0=2\times V_2\] \[V_2=0
\tag{iii}\] Substituting (ii) and (iii) into (i), \[V_{C_1}=V_{C_2}
=
\frac{6\times1+0\times2}{1+2}
=
\frac{6}{3}
=
2~\text{V}\] Hence, \[Q_{2(\text{steady state})}
=
C_2V_{C_2}
=
2\times2
=
4~\text{C}\] Therefore, \[\boxed{4}\]

Given: \[x[n]=e^{j\left(\frac{5\pi}{3}\right)n}+e^{j\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)n}\] For down-sampling by \(4\), \[n=4n\] \[x[4n]
=
e^{j\left(\frac{5\pi}{3}\right)4n}
+
e^{j\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)4n}\] \[\omega_1=\frac{20\pi}{3},
\qquad
\omega_2=\pi\] \[N_1=\frac{2\pi k}{20\pi/3}
=
\frac{3k}{10}\] Putting \(k=10\), \[N_1=3\] \[N_2=\frac{2\pi k}{\pi}
=
