=
6\] Hence, the fundamental period of the down-sampled signal \(x_d[n]\) is \(6\). 6

Given that the current through the left \(3\,\text{k}\Omega\) resistor is zero, \[I=0.\] <img src="img/8cbbad3ac82696a8.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Hence, there is no voltage drop across this resistor and the left node voltage is \[V_{A}=1\,\text{V}.\] The first op-amp is an inverting amplifier with \[R_{in}=R_f=3\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad V_{+}=0.\] Therefore, \[V_{o1}
=-\frac{R_f}{R_{in}}V_A
=-1\,\text{V}.\] The second op-amp is also an inverting amplifier with \[R_{in}=3\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
R_f=12\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Hence, \[V_{o2}
=-\frac{12}{3}V_{o1}
=-4(-1)=4\,\text{V}.\] Applying KCL at node \(A\), \[\frac{1-0}{3\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{1-4}{R}=0.\] Thus, \[\frac{1}{3000}=\frac{3}{R},\] \[R=9000\,\Omega=9\,\text{k}\Omega.\] \[\boxed{9}\]

The non-inverting terminal is grounded. Hence, for an ideal op-amp, \[V_{-}=0,\] i.e., the inverting terminal is at virtual ground. <img src="img/c450514e193b2fe1.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Therefore, the diode voltage is \[V_D=V_{in}.\] The diode current is \[I=I_0e^{V_D/V_T}
=I_0e^{V_{in}/V_T}.\] Since the op-amp input current is zero, the same current flows through the feedback resistor: \[I=\frac{0-V_{out}}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}.\] Hence, \[V_{out}
=-1000\,I_0e^{V_{in}/V_T}.\] Thus, \[V_{out}\propto e^{V_{in}/V_T}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) }e^{V_{in}/V_T}}\]

Since the disturbance is added at the output, \[\frac{Y(s)}{D(s)}=\frac{1}{1+L(s)}\] From the Bode magnitude plot, \[20\log|L(j5)|=40\,\mathrm{dB}\] \[|L(j5)|=10^{40/20}=100\] Hence, \[\left|\frac{Y(j5)}{D(j5)}\right|
=\frac{1}{|1+L(j5)|}
\approx\frac{1}{|L(j5)|}
=\frac{1}{100}, \qquad \qquad \text{since } |L(j5)|\gg1\] The disturbance amplitude is, \(|D|=0.1.\) Therefore, the steady-state output amplitude is, \(|Y|
=0.1\times\dfrac{1}{100}
=10^{-3}\) \[\boxed{(A)\;1.00\times10^{-3}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>If the input to a transfer function \(G(s)\) is \(x(t)=A\sin(\omega_0 t+\phi),\) then the steady-state output is: \[y(t)=A|G(j\omega_0)|\sin\!\left(\omega_0 t+\phi+\angle G(j\omega_0)\right)\]</li><li>The amplitude of the steady state output is, \(A|G(j\omega_0)|\) and the phase is, \(\phi+\angle G(j\omega_0)\)</li></ul></div>

